<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Clinical development principles">
  <div><strong>Research is not care</strong><span>A study protocol answers a research question. It does not replace individualized diagnosis, treatment or a clinician's responsibility to the patient.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Study type matters</strong><span>Observational and interventional studies have different methods, participant burdens and regulatory implications.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Authorization has boundaries</strong><span>A Natural Product Number authorizes sale under labelled conditions of use. It does not automatically establish every proposed clinical application.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why clinical development exists</p>
    <h2>Clinical questions need evidence from the people, setting and outcome that matter</h2>
    <p>Hydration physiology can explain why fluid, sodium, potassium, chloride and glucose are biologically relevant. That is not the same as demonstrating that a specific product improves a defined clinical outcome in a defined patient population.</p>
    <p>Clinical development closes that gap deliberately. It starts by defining the use context and measurement problem, then asks what observational, feasibility or interventional evidence would actually be needed before a broader clinical statement is justified.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Development pathway</p>
  <h2>From clinical question to responsible application</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Define the clinical problem</h3><p>Specify population, setting, hydration challenge, existing care pathway and the exact outcome the research is intended to understand.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Characterize baseline and feasibility</h3><p>Use literature, workflow mapping or appropriately governed observational work to understand variability, measurement burden and practical constraints.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Write the protocol before the result</h3><p>Predefine intervention or exposure, comparator where appropriate, outcomes, sample timing, safety monitoring, analysis and stopping criteria.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Obtain the applicable approvals</h3><p>Human-participant research may require Research Ethics Board review, informed consent and, for applicable natural health product clinical trials, Health Canada authorization before commencement.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Conduct, analyze and report</h3><p>Preserve data integrity, report adverse events where required, analyze according to the protocol and separate exploratory findings from prespecified outcomes.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Translate only what was demonstrated</h3><p>Update education, product development or future study design without extending conclusions beyond the studied formulation, dose, population and care context.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4266943/pexels-photo-4266943.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Doctor consulting with a patient using a digital tablet in a clinic" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Research versus care</p>
    <h2>The participant and the patient are not interchangeable roles</h2>
    <p>Clinical care is organized around the needs of an individual patient. Research is organized around a protocol designed to produce generalizable knowledge. The two can occur in the same institution, but the purpose, consent process, data use and decision-making framework are different.</p>
    <p>For Best Hydrate, that means a research page should never read like a treatment recommendation. Eligibility for a study does not mean a product is clinically indicated for every person with a similar condition, and participation should not displace appropriate medical assessment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Study design</p>
  <h2>Observational and interventional research answer different questions</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Observational research</th><th>Interventional clinical research</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Assignment</strong></td><td>Researchers observe exposures or care that are not assigned by the study.</td><td>Investigators prospectively assign an intervention or strategy according to a protocol.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best use</strong></td><td>Describe patterns, feasibility, associations, natural variation or real-world workflows.</td><td>Evaluate safety, tolerability, biological effects or health outcomes attributable to a defined intervention.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Causal strength</strong></td><td>Often limited by confounding and selection effects.</td><td>Can support stronger causal inference when design, control and execution are appropriate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Regulatory point</strong></td><td>Health Canada's NHP guidance describes a narrow observational category that does not manipulate treatment or collect samples outside normal care. REB review still applies in the circumstances described by that guidance.</td><td>Applicable Phase I to III natural health product trials require Health Canada authorization before commencement, in addition to research ethics requirements.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Canadian regulatory context</p>
    <h2>Natural health product licensing and clinical-trial authorization are different regulatory questions</h2>
    <p>Health Canada identifies licensed natural health products with an eight-digit Natural Product Number. An NPN means the product has been authorized for sale in Canada and is considered safe and effective when used according to the instructions on the label.</p>
    <p>A clinical trial can ask a different question, for example a different population, endpoint, protocol or condition of use. Health Canada's Natural Health Product clinical-trial guidance therefore has a separate authorization pathway for applicable trials, including Phase I to III studies.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate's NPN should therefore be presented as market authorization under labelled conditions, not as a shortcut to broader clinical claims that have not been studied or authorized.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Regulatory distinction</span>
    <h3>NPN ≠ proof of every proposed clinical use.</h3>
    <p>Product licensing, clinical-trial authorization, research ethics approval and evidence for a specific clinical claim are related but distinct.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

