<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Research ethics</p>
    <h2>Consent must be free, informed and ongoing</h2>
    <p>Canada's Tri-Council Policy Statement describes consent in research involving humans as free, informed and ongoing. Participants should understand the purpose of the research, what participation involves, foreseeable risks and potential benefits, and their right to make a voluntary decision.</p>
    <p>That principle is especially important when research occurs near clinical care, where patients may otherwise assume that a study intervention is being recommended primarily for their individual therapeutic benefit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Transparency</p>
  <h2>Registration and results reporting are part of modern clinical development</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
    <article class="bhd-page__prose">
      <p>Health Canada's guidance on clinical-trial registration and public disclosure of results became effective July 29, 2026 and applies to regulated health products including natural health products. The guidance is intended to improve public access to information about trials and their results.</p>
      <p>As Best Hydrate moves from exploratory methods work into any applicable regulated clinical trial, public registration, status updates and results disclosure should be incorporated into the protocol and publication plan rather than treated as an afterthought.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
      <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Transparency rule</span>
      <h3>Register the question before celebrating the answer.</h3>
      <p>Prospective protocol clarity and public reporting reduce the risk of selectively highlighting only favorable findings.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

